From eabe3e2d8fae67829999947ed690e4de47384a29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:43:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Load share package binaries prebuilt beside the Lisp
 image

Share packages built with MK:DEFSYSTEM (draw, lapack, linearalgebra,
...) compile their Lisp files into each user's maxima_objdir when first
loaded.  That makes a first test suite run slower than later ones, lets
parallel runs race on the same files, and needs a C compiler with ECL
or GCL.  Binaries put anywhere else were never found.

lisp-utils/defsystem.lisp: a :BINARY-PATHNAME form may return a second
value, a read-only directory of prebuilt binaries laid out like the
binary root.  OPERATE-ON-SYSTEM takes all binaries of a system from
there (PREBUILT-BINARY-ROOT) when every file of the system has a binary
there that is not older than its source; otherwise the system compiles
into its own binary root as before.  COMPONENT-FULL-PATHNAME maps the
root.  LOAD-FILE-OPERATION recompiles a prebuilt binary that fails to
load into the binary root instead of deleting it.

src/init-cl.lisp: MAXIMA-OBJDIR returns the same subdirectory of
*MAXIMA-PREBUILT-OBJDIR*, the image directory, as its second value, so
no .system file or share loader changes.  maxima -d prints it.

"make share-binaries" compiles the packages into
src/binary-<lisp>/share and checks that loading them then compiles
nothing.  "make install" installs them after share/, so that none is
older than its installed source, and refuses them if a share source
changed since.

share/lapack/load-lapack.lisp: no heap warning when a prebuilt dgesvd
binary exists.

